<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vladimir the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 958–1015</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Vladimir I of Kiev, Volodymyr the Great, Saint Vladimir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vladimir the Great was Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> from about 980 to 1015. In 988 he accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and married the emperor's sister Anna, bringing churches, Church Slavonic writing and Byzantine art to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>. He is venerated as a saint in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Catholic churches.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great.jpg" alt="Vladimir the Great" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Влад Федченко · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Vladimir_the_Great.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
